Subject: [PATCH] net: macb: restart the transmit queues after a link flap

The GEM driver can stall a transmit queue for good when the link goes
down and up while frames are still in its ring.  On link-up,
gem_shuffle_tx_one_ring() rotates each ring so that the oldest
unfinished frame sits at index 0, and macb_mac_link_up() enables the
transmitter.  Nothing restarts the queue after that: the stack holds it
stopped on the bytes still in flight, so no new frame comes to write
TSTART, and the frames behind it are never sent.  Measured on the Zynq
UltraScale+ GEM (two queues) and the Zynq-7000 GEM (one), each under
saturating load across a restart of autonegotiation.

1. TBQP, the controller's pointer into the ring, is not set back to the
   ring base, and can stand past the pending frames: measured at
   link-up, one queue's pointer at index 397 with its pending frames at
   387 to 396.  The first two hunks write TBQP to the ring base on every
   path that sets the tail to 0.  They are the upstream patch "net: macb:
   reprogram TBQP after shuffling the TX ring on link-up" (netdev, July
   2026, https://lkml.iu.edu/2607.0/11020.html; Fixes: 881a0263d502
   "net: macb: Shuffle the tx ring before enabling tx"), unchanged but
   for this kernel's line numbers.

2. TSTART is not written, so with the pointer at the frames the
   transmitter stays idle.  The last hunk writes TSTART once after
   enabling the transmitter if any queue still holds frames.  It is not
   from upstream.

Measured on the UltraScale+ GEM: without this patch, and with the first
half alone, both queues stalled at the first flap; with the second half
alone 80 flaps of 80 drained, and with both 100 of 100.

Not fixed: a third state, in which after the restarted frames were sent
the pointer is back at the ring base on a used descriptor, behind the
tail, and TXUBR interrupts come at 100,000 to 250,000 a second.  On the
UltraScale+ GEM it follows when the link-down is handled 20 ms later
than it is here (a delay added before TE is cleared), with or without
this patch.  On the Zynq-7000 GEM it came at the third flap with this
patch (and without it the queue stalled at the second, idle at the
ring base), and also with the transmitter halted before TE is cleared.

Applied to Buildroot's mainline kernel by BR2_GLOBAL_PATCH_DIR.
---
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/cadence/macb_main.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/cadence/macb_main.c
@@ -697,7 +697,7 @@ static void gem_shuffle_tx_one_ring(stru
 	if (!count) {
 		queue->tx_head = 0;
 		queue->tx_tail = 0;
-		goto unlock;
+		goto reset_hw_ptr;
 	}
 
 	shift = tail % ring_size;
@@ -746,6 +746,13 @@ static void gem_shuffle_tx_one_ring(stru
 	/* Make descriptor updates visible to hardware */
 	wmb();
 
+reset_hw_ptr:
+	/* tx_tail was reset to the ring base, so TBQP must be reprogrammed
+	 * to match; otherwise it keeps pointing at a stale descriptor. Safe
+	 * to write directly here as TX is still disabled (called from
+	 * macb_mac_link_up() before TE is set).
+	 */
+	queue_writel(queue, TBQP, lower_32_bits(queue->tx_ring_dma));
 unlock:
 	spin_unlock_irqrestore(&queue->tx_ptr_lock, flags);
 }
@@ -823,6 +830,27 @@ static void macb_mac_link_up(struct phyl
 
 	macb_writel(bp, NCR, ctrl | MACB_BIT(RE) | MACB_BIT(TE));
 
+	/* Frames queued before the link went down are still in the rings,
+	 * now at their bases, and the transmitter was enabled idle: start it,
+	 * since no new frame may come to do so while the stack holds the
+	 * queues stopped on those frames' bytes.
+	 */
+	if (!(bp->caps & MACB_CAPS_MACB_IS_EMAC)) {
+		bool pending = false;
+
+		for (q = 0, queue = bp->queues; q < bp->num_queues; ++q, ++queue) {
+			spin_lock_irqsave(&queue->tx_ptr_lock, flags);
+			if (queue->tx_head != queue->tx_tail)
+				pending = true;
+			spin_unlock_irqrestore(&queue->tx_ptr_lock, flags);
+		}
+		if (pending) {
+			spin_lock_irqsave(&bp->lock, flags);
+			macb_writel(bp, NCR, macb_readl(bp, NCR) | MACB_BIT(TSTART));
+			spin_unlock_irqrestore(&bp->lock, flags);
+		}
+	}
+
 	netif_tx_wake_all_queues(ndev);
 }
 
